# Private Traefik gotchas

- Isolation is by class annotation: private IngressRoutes carry
  `kubernetes.io/ingress.class: traefik-internal`. The public instance's
  kubernetescrd provider class is unset, so it processes only routes with no
  class or class `traefik` and ignores annotated ones. The private instance
  sets `providers.kubernetesCRD.ingressClass: traefik-internal`.
- The chart's pod label `app.kubernetes.io/instance` is `<release>-<namespace>`
  (`traefik-internal-traefik-internal`), not just the release name. Service
  selectors and affinity/spread selectors must use the full value.
- The private app renders no IngressClass, TLSStore, TLSOption, Service, or
  CRDs (`service.enabled: false`, `ingressClass.enabled: false`,
  `helm.skipCrds: true`; the public app owns those).
- Each private service needs a pinned ClusterIP from `10.43.0.128/25` and its
  own Traefik entrypoint port: NetBird policies are L3/L4, so services sharing
  a ClusterIP or port are indistinguishable to enforcement. The /25 sits in
  the KEP-3070 static band of the `10.43.0.0/16` service CIDR, which the
  dynamic allocator never assigns from.
- The operator's `NetworkResource` with `serviceRef` creates an IP resource at
  the Service ClusterIP plus an A record in `svc.olympus.yggdrasil.mia.cx`.
- New private namespaces must be added to
  `providers.kubernetesCRD.namespaces` in `values.yaml`; the instance watches
  nothing else.

